<h2>Writing the five-email sequence</h2>

<p>There is no single right way to structure a drip campaign, but the five-email sequence below is a proven framework that works across industries. You can adjust it, shorten it, or extend it, but this gives you a strong foundation to build from.</p>

<h2>Email 1: The welcome (Day 0)</h2>

<p>This is the most important email in your sequence. It has the highest open rate of any email you will ever send, often north of 60%. Do not waste it.</p>

<p>Your welcome email should do three things:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Deliver on your promise.</strong> If they signed up for a free guide, give them the download link immediately. If they requested a quote, confirm it is on its way. Do not make people hunt for what they were promised.</li>
  <li><strong>Set expectations.</strong> Tell them what they will receive from you and how often. "Over the next couple of weeks, I will send you a few emails with practical tips on [topic]" removes the mystery and reduces unsubscribes.</li>
  <li><strong>Introduce yourself briefly.</strong> One or two sentences about who you are and why you care about this topic. Keep it human. Nobody wants a corporate biography.</li>
</ul>

<p>The welcome email is also your chance to create a strong <a href="/blog/customer-follow-up-system">follow-up system</a> from the very first interaction. First impressions in email are hard to undo, so get this one right.</p>

<h2>Email 2: The value email (Day 2)</h2>

<p>Two days after the welcome, send something genuinely useful. This email is not about you. It is about them.</p>

<p>Share a practical tip, a quick win, or a piece of insight that they can apply immediately. The goal is to build trust by being helpful without asking for anything in return. Think of it as a deposit into the relationship bank account. You are going to make withdrawals later, so build up the balance now.</p>

<p>Good examples include:</p>

<ul>
  <li>A checklist they can use straight away</li>
  <li>A common mistake in their industry and how to avoid it</li>
  <li>A short case study showing a result (without pitching your product)</li>
  <li>A useful resource, tool, or framework</li>
</ul>

<p>Keep it focused. One idea per email. If you try to cover too much ground, nothing sticks.</p>

<h2>Email 3: The soft pitch (Day 5)</h2>

<p>By day five, your subscriber has received value from you twice without being asked to buy anything. They are starting to trust you. Now you can begin to introduce your solution, gently.</p>

<p>The soft pitch works best as a story. Share a case study or a customer success story that shows how your product or service solved the exact problem your subscriber is dealing with. Let the results speak for themselves. You are not saying "buy this." You are saying "here is what happened when someone in your position tried this approach."</p>

<p>Include a link to your offer or a relevant <a href="/blog/landing-pages-that-convert">landing page</a>, but do not make it the centrepiece of the email. The story is the centrepiece. The link is there for people who are ready to take the next step.</p>

<h2>Email 4: The direct offer (Day 8)</h2>

<p>This is where you make your pitch clearly and directly. No hiding behind stories. No burying the call to action at the bottom. This email says: here is what we offer, here is why it matters, and here is how to get started.</p>

<p>Structure it simply:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Name the problem.</strong> Remind them of the pain point they are dealing with.</li>
  <li><strong>Present your solution.</strong> Explain what you offer and how it solves that problem.</li>
  <li><strong>Show the proof.</strong> A testimonial, a result, or a specific number that backs up your claim.</li>
  <li><strong>Make the CTA unmistakable.</strong> One clear call to action. Not three links to three different things. One button. One next step.</li>
</ul>

<p>People respect directness when it follows genuine value. You have earned the right to ask by now.</p>

<h2>Email 5: The urgency close (Day 12)</h2>

<p>The final email in your sequence creates a reason to act now rather than later. "Later" is where good intentions go to die. If you do not give people a reason to decide, most of them simply will not.</p>

<p>Effective urgency tactics include:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A deadline.</strong> "This offer expires on Friday" is simple and effective. Make sure the deadline is real.</li>
  <li><strong>Scarcity.</strong> "We only take on five new clients each month" works if it is true. Never manufacture fake scarcity.</li>
  <li><strong>Social proof.</strong> "Over 200 businesses signed up last quarter" reassures people they are not taking a risk by choosing you.</li>
</ul>

<p>Combine two of these elements for maximum impact. A deadline paired with social proof is powerful. A scarcity claim backed by a testimonial is convincing. The key is honesty. Fake urgency destroys trust faster than almost anything else in marketing.</p>

<blockquote><p>Urgency without trust is spam. Trust without urgency is a missed opportunity. Your drip campaign should build one before deploying the other.</p></blockquote>

