<h2>Getting the timing right</h2>

<p>The spacing between emails matters more than most people think. Too close together and you feel pushy. Too far apart and they forget who you are.</p>

<p>Here is a timing framework that works well for most small businesses:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Email 1 (Welcome):</strong> Immediately after signup, or within minutes</li>
  <li><strong>Email 2 (Value):</strong> Day 2</li>
  <li><strong>Email 3 (Soft pitch):</strong> Day 5</li>
  <li><strong>Email 4 (Direct offer):</strong> Day 8</li>
  <li><strong>Email 5 (Urgency close):</strong> Day 12</li>
</ul>

<p>Notice the gaps get wider as the sequence progresses. Early on, you are building momentum and familiarity. Later, you are giving people space to think and decide. This rhythm feels natural rather than aggressive.</p>

<p>You can adjust these intervals based on your industry and sales cycle. A low-cost impulse purchase might compress the whole sequence into a week. A high-ticket service with a longer decision cycle might stretch it to three or four weeks. Test and adjust based on your open and click rates.</p>

<h2>Subject lines that get your emails opened</h2>

<p>None of this matters if people do not open your emails. The subject line is the gatekeeper, and getting it right is a skill worth developing.</p>

<p>Four principles that consistently drive higher open rates:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Curiosity.</strong> Make them want to know more. "The follow-up mistake that costs most businesses thousands" is more compelling than "Tips for better follow-ups."</li>
  <li><strong>Specificity.</strong> Vague subject lines get ignored. "3 ways to cut your booking no-shows by half" outperforms "How to reduce no-shows" because the specificity signals real, actionable content.</li>
  <li><strong>Personalisation.</strong> Using the subscriber's name or referencing their specific situation increases opens. Even simple touches like "[First name], quick question for you" feel more personal than broadcast-style subject lines.</li>
  <li><strong>Length.</strong> Keep subject lines under 50 characters when possible. Most people read email on their phones, and anything longer gets cut off mid-sentence. If your subject line needs a second line to make sense, it is too long.</li>
</ul>

<p>Avoid spam triggers like ALL CAPS, excessive exclamation marks, and words like "free," "guaranteed," or "act now." These do not just annoy readers. They trigger spam filters and your email never reaches the inbox at all.</p>

<h2>Segmentation: different drips for different people</h2>

<p>Sending the same drip campaign to every subscriber is better than sending nothing, but it is not where the real results live. The real results come from segmentation, which means sending different sequences to different groups based on who they are and what they need.</p>

<p>You do not need dozens of segments. Start with two or three based on the most obvious differences in your audience:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>By lead source.</strong> Someone who downloaded a pricing guide is closer to buying than someone who downloaded a general educational resource. They should get different sequences.</li>
  <li><strong>By service interest.</strong> If you offer multiple services, segment based on which one they enquired about. A drip campaign about kitchen renovations is wasted on someone looking for bathroom work.</li>
  <li><strong>By customer stage.</strong> New leads, existing customers, and lapsed customers all need different messaging. A welcome sequence makes no sense for someone who has already bought from you three times.</li>
</ul>

<p>Even basic segmentation can double or triple your conversion rates compared to a one-size-fits-all approach. As your list grows and your data improves, you can add more sophisticated segments over time.</p>

<h2>Measuring and optimising your results</h2>

<p>Once your drip campaign is live, pay attention to three metrics that tell you whether it is working.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Open rate.</strong> This tells you whether your subject lines are doing their job. A healthy open rate for drip campaigns sits between 25% and 45%. If you are below 20%, your subject lines need work or your list quality is poor.</li>
  <li><strong>Click-through rate.</strong> This measures whether the content inside your emails is compelling enough to drive action. Aim for 3% to 7% on most emails in your sequence. Your direct offer emails should be at the higher end.</li>
  <li><strong>Conversion rate.</strong> This is the number that actually matters. How many people who enter your drip campaign end up taking the action you want? For most small businesses, a 1% to 5% end-to-end conversion rate from a drip campaign is solid. Above 5% is excellent.</li>
</ul>

<p>Review these numbers monthly. If one email in your sequence has a significantly lower open rate than the others, rewrite the subject line. If clicks drop off at email three, the content might not be resonating. If people open and click but do not convert, your offer or landing page needs attention.</p>

<p>Small, consistent improvements compound over time. A drip campaign you refine every month for six months will dramatically outperform one you set and forget.</p>

<blockquote><p>The best drip campaigns are never truly finished. They are living systems that you test, refine, and improve based on what the data tells you.</p></blockquote>

<h2>Putting it all together</h2>

<p>Building a drip campaign that converts is not about writing clever copy or finding the perfect automation tool. It is about understanding what your customer needs at each stage of their journey and delivering it at the right time, in the right order, without relying on memory or manual effort.</p>

<p>Start with the five-email framework. Map it to your customer journey. Write subject lines that earn the open. Segment your audience so the right people get the right message. Then measure, adjust, and improve.</p>

<p>The businesses that do this well do not just generate more leads. They convert more of the leads they already have, which is almost always the cheaper and faster path to growth.</p>

